WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.620
[music]

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.100
Hi, I'm Meghan Rosen,

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.459
and I'm a staff reporter at Science News.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:12.520
So, I cover everything from robots to dinosaurs,

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.440
but this year I spent a lot of time

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.720
focusing on the Zika virus outbreak.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:19.090
So this year we've learned a lot about

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.660
how Zika virus can affect baby's brain and body,

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.340
but we don't yet have a vaccine.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.860
So I want to tell you about two approaches

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.520
to vaccine making that could

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.420
have some interesting results in 2017.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:32.120
The first approach is being used by the Army

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:33.540
at Walter Reed,

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.880
and basically they're making something called

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.880
the Zika PIV vaccine.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.700
And so that stands for purified inactivated virus.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.120
And what you do here is

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.540
grow up a big batch of Zika virus,

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.140
and then you kill it with chemicals, and then you purify it,

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:50.720
and that's basically your vaccine.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.900
So it sounds pretty easy

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.680
and it's a classic way to make a vaccine --

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.600
it goes all the way back to Jonas Salk and Polio.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:59.580
But there's one big drawback,

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.080
and that's it can take a lot of time and money

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:05.800
to grow up all the virus you need to make the vaccine.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.840
So another approach to making a vaccine for Zika virus

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.340
is one that's being used by the NIH.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.500
Instead of using a killed virus,

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.020
they're actually using something

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:16.880
called a DNA-based vaccine.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.380
And so you use tiny snippets of DNA

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.420
and they encode for Zika virus proteins.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:24.860
So this is considered a much

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.020
faster way to make a vaccine,

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:28.820
because you don't have to grow up

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:30.600
those huge batches of virus.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.120
But there's one big caveat,

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:33.660
the FDA has never approved

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.660
a DNA-based vaccine in humans.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.220
Both kinds of vaccines have already started

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.380
working their way through human safety trials.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.940
So I'm hoping that we see some results in 2017.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.100
I think the earliest we can expect to see

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.640
a vaccine for Zika is in 2018 or 2019.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.300
Two or three years might seem like

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.300
a long time to wait for a Zika vaccine,

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.380
but in terms of vaccine development,

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.100
it's a really short timeline.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.760
Until 2015 Zika was considered a really obscure virus,

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:02.980
so it's been really interesting this year to follow

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.460
the science and to learn something new everyday.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.160
[music]

